  "commentary_text": "In order to achieve a larger SR for the same GBW, we take the ratio and rewrite it in terms of transistor parameters. We find that for a MOST, this ratio is simply proportional to its V −V . The GS T larger we make the input transistor V −V , the GS1 T larger the SR will be for the same GBW. Clearly, SR has to do with high speed. This result is not unexpected. We have known all along that for high speed we need to take large values of V −V . The noise performance will suffer from that, but again we have GS1 T known this all along. Clearly, the use of a bipolar transistor at the input reduces the SR by a factor of 10 for the same GBW. This also applies to a MOST in weak inversion. In order to improve the SR for a bipolar amplifier, we have to insert series resistors. The SR increases accordingly. Clearly, the noise performance suffers from that as well. To realize an amplifier with high speed or SR, and low noise at the same time is a real compromise!",
